        crop_name='Apple',
        crop_group='Fruits',
        season='Planting is done late in winter. For adequate root development a temperature of 7oC is considered ideal.',
        varieties=[{'name': 'Rootstocks', 'description': 'Apples are ordinarily propagated by budding or grafting on seedlings of Crab apple, Yellow Newton or Golden Delicious. Low chilling varieties are suitable for Kerala.'}],
        spacing='The planting distance in India varies from 7-10 m, depending upon the vigour of plant.',
        manuring_fertilizer='It is desirable to add 100-150 g of nitrogen as sulphate of ammonia. Similar quantities of phosphate and potash should be added when required. Five quintals of bone meal and 10 quintals of wood ash per hectare are given annually besides the fertilizers. Fertilizers should be mixed with the soil at a radius of 1 m from the plant.',
        plant_protection='',
        harvesting='Much of the success in apple production depends on proper picking, storage and disposal. When a fruit separates easily from the spur, firmness of flesh and taste are desirable. The harvested fruits may be stored for 120-150 days at 4-5oC, provided there is good circulation of air.',
        expected_yield='',
        sections=[{'body': 'Apple is an important temperate fruit suited for growing in the high ranges of Kerala. Sloping sites to allow free drainage are considered more suitable than level tops. The ideal soil types are loam, sandy loam and silt loam with open porous and well-drained subsoil.', 'heading': 'Overview'}, {'body': 'The pit should be of 1 m wide and 20 cm deep so that all roots may be accommodated in a well spread condition.', 'heading': 'Preparation of land'}, {'body': "Winters are best for whip grafting. Shield budding is done in June with the season's bud. Both whip grafting and shield budding are widely practiced in India.", 'heading': 'Planting material'}, {'body': 'Apples are planted in the ground free of weeds. Regularly irrigated for about two years in the beginning.', 'heading': 'Time and method of planting'}, {'body': 'Pruning and training are important in apple cultivation. One year old plants are cut back at about 80-100 cm above ground. If branches are present at this time, only 4 to 5 of them ought to be retained and shortened in length. No shoot is retained upto 50 cm from ground. At the time of first dormant pruning, the main scaffold branches are cut back to about half a metre in length. Secondary branches arise from these main limbs. Some of the new shoots arising early in the second summer are rubbed off in order to develop only a few vigorous secondary branches. During the second dormant pruning, the crowded, misplaced or diseased secondary branches are removed and the extra vigorous ones headed back. This process is continued for 4 or 5 years, at the end of which there are 8 to 10 scaffold branches.', 'heading': 'Pruning'}, {'body': 'Thinning of fruit is also practised in order to improve fruit colour and fruit size. It is desirable to retain one fruit for every 40 leaves. This spaces the fruit at about 15-20 cm apart and there will be only one fruit per spur.', 'heading': 'Thinning of fruit'}],
        source_reference='KAU Package of Practices Recommendations: Crops 2024 (16th ed.)',
