    dict(
        crop_name='French bean',
        crop_group='Pulses',
        season='In the high ranges of elevation more than 1000 m, this crop can be grown throughout the year. The crop being susceptible to ground frost in higher altitudes (above 1400 m), adequate protection should be given during January-February.',
        varieties=[{'name': 'Pole beans', 'description': 'Kentucky Wonder'}, {'name': 'Bush beans', 'description': 'Contender, Premier, YCD-1, Arka Komal, Tender Green'}],
        spacing='Spacing of 30 cm x 20 cm is recommended. For pole beans, raised beds are advantageous.',
        manuring_fertilizer='Apply basal dose of 20 t/ha of FYM and N:P2O5:K2O @ 30:40:60 kg/ha. Top dressing with 30 kg N/ha may be given 20 days after sowing.',
        plant_protection='',
        harvesting='Pods become ready for harvest in 50-60 days in the case of bush beans and in 70-80 days for the pole beans. The average yield of green pods is 8-10 t/ha.',
        expected_yield='8-10 t/ha (green pods)',
        sections=[{'body': 'French bean (Phaseolus vulgaris)\n\nLight sandy-loam to clayey-loam soils with good drainage are best suited for the crop. There are two types of French beans viz., pole beans and bush beans.', 'heading': 'Overview'}, {'body': '80 kg/ha for hills; 50 kg/ha for plains.', 'heading': 'Seed rate'}, {'body': 'Prepare land thoroughly by ploughing. Raised beds are not essential for bush beans. For pole beans, raised beds are advantageous.', 'heading': 'Sowing'}, {'body': 'Provide support, 1 to 1.5 m long for trailing the plants. First weeding can be given about 4 weeks after sowing and second weeding will be essential 50 days later.', 'heading': 'After cultivation'}],
        source_reference='KAU Package of Practices Recommendations: Crops 2024 (16th ed.)',
        source_page_range='64-65',
        is_active=True,
    ),
    dict(
        crop_name='Gamba grass',
        crop_group='Fodder Crops',
        season='',
        varieties=[],
        spacing='',
        manuring_fertilizer='',
        plant_protection='',
        harvesting='',
        expected_yield='In general about 50 to 80 t ha-1 of green matter is produced in the first year. From the second year onwards there is a slight increase in green fodder yield.',
        sections=[{'body': "Gamba grass is also known as 'Sadabahar'. It is a tufted perennial grass and the stems are usually 1-2 m high. The inflorescence is a large spathe or panicle. The grass tolerates drought and suits areas where dry season lasts for five months or so. In areas with less severe drought it can remain green throughout the year. It tolerates deep seasonal flooding. The grass avoids heavy soil, is resistant to grass fires and develops new leaves and shoots a few days after burning. The crop comes up well in partial shade and is a good intercrop in coconut gardens.", 'heading': 'Overview'}, {'body': 'The crop can be propagated through rooted slips or seeds lightly drilled. Cultural operations and management are similar to that of guinea grass.', 'heading': 'Propagation'}, {'body': 'The grass has excellent palatability with 5.5 per cent crude protein and 32.6 per cent crude fibre.', 'heading': 'Quality'}],
        source_reference='KAU Package of Practices Recommendations: Crops 2024 (16th ed.)',
        source_page_range='289 (book p 283)',
        is_active=True,
    ),
    dict(
        crop_name='Garlic',
        crop_group='Vegetables',
        season='In high ranges of Kerala garlic can be planted during April-May or October-November.',
        varieties=[{'name': 'Ooty-1, G 50'}],
        spacing='The cloves should be dibbled at a spacing of 15 cm x 8 cm.',
        manuring_fertilizer='Apply N:P2O5:K2O @ 60:120:120 kg/ha 20 days after transplanting (DAP). Top dressing of N should be done @ 60 kg/ha, 45 DAP.',
        plant_protection='',
        harvesting='Harvesting can be done 90-130 DAP depending upon the variety used for cultivation. Yield may vary from 5-10 t/ha.',
        expected_yield='5-10 t/ha',
        sections=[{'body': 'Garlic (Allium sativum)\n\nGarlic requires cool and moist period during vegetative growth and a dry spell during maturity of the bulbs. Fertile, well-drained loamy soils are ideal for garlic cultivation. Heavy clay soils may result in deformed bulbs.', 'heading': 'Overview'}, {'body': 'Cloves or bulbils are used for propagation. For planting one hectare, 500 kg of cloves is required. The cloves for planting should be stored for 2-3 months after harvest and cloves weighing 4 g are ideal for planting. The cloves should be soaked in water followed by dipping for 15 minutes in a solution containing 1 g of carbendazim dissolved in 1 litre of water for 15 minutes. After drying in shade, cloves can be used for planting. Dig the land thoroughly and prepare beds of 15 cm height at a width of 1 m and of convenient length. The cloves should be dibbled at a spacing of 15 cm x 8 cm. Germination will start on the fifth day and it will be completed within 10-15 days.', 'heading': 'Planting requirements'}, {'body': 'Earthing up should be done 60 DAP.', 'heading': 'After cultivation'}],
        source_reference='KAU Package of Practices Recommendations: Crops 2024 (16th ed.)',
        source_page_range='231-232',
        is_active=True,
    ),
    dict(
        crop_name='Ginger',
        crop_group='Spices and Condiments',
        season='The best time for planting ginger is during the first fortnight of April, after receipt of pre-monsoon showers. For irrigated ginger, the best suited time for planting is middle of February (for vegetable ginger).',
        varieties=[{'name': 'Improved varieties', 'description': 'Athira, Karthika, Aswathy, IISR-Varada, IISR-Rejatha and IISR-Mahima.'}, {'name': 'Dry ginger Cultivars', 'description': 'Maran, Wayanad, Manantoddy, Himachal, Valluvanad, Kuruppampady.'}, {'name': 'Green ginger cultivars/varieties', 'description': 'Rio-De-Janeiro, China, Wayanad Local and Aswathy.'}, {'name': 'Dual purpose varieties', 'description': 'Athira, Karthika, IISR-Varada, IISR-Rejatha and IISR-Mahima.'}, {'name': 'KAU Chandra', 'description': 'Crude fibre 3.0%, girth of fingers 7.11 cm; driage 22.26%; volatile oil 1.6% and oleoresin 5.17%. Good for fresh and dry ginger. Suitable for cultivation in moist humus rich soils of tropics.'}, {'name': 'KAU Chitra', 'description': 'Extra bold fingers 8.3 cm diameter, low fibre 3.01%, high driage 23.4%; volatile oil 1.6% and oleoresin 4.71%. Ideal for dry ginger. Suitable for cultivation in moist humus rich soils of tropics.'}],
        spacing='Plant rhizome bits of 15 g weight in small pits at a spacing of 20 cm x 20 cm to 25 cm x 25 cm and at a depth of 4-5 cm with at least one viable healthy bud facing upwards. Seed rate 1500 kg/ha.',
        manuring_fertilizer='Apply 20 t FYM + 2 t Neem cake + 1 t Ash + 4 t vermicompost/ha. Also use Azospirillum and P-Solubilising bacteria (20 g/bed of 3 x 1 m) as nutrient supplement. Drench the beds with PGPR GRB 35 (Bacillus amyloliquifacians) for growth promotion and suppression of soft rot disease.\n\nApply manures and fertilizers at the following rates: FYM 30 t/ha; N:P2O5:K2O 75:50:50 kg/ha/year. Full dose of P2O5 and 50 per cent of K2O may be applied as basal. Half the quantity of N may be applied 60 days after planting. The remaining quantity of N and K2O may be applied 120 days after planting.\n\nMicronutrient formulation for foliar spray in ginger: To correct micronutrient deficiency, dissolve IISR Ginger special @ 5 g/l water (1 kg per 200 L barrel) and apply as foliar spray twice at 60 days after planting and 90 days after planting.\n\nMulching: Immediately after planting, mulch the beds thickly with green leaves @ 15 t/ha. Repeat mulching with green leaves twice @ 7.5 t/ha first 44-60 days and second 90-120 days after planting. Grow green manure crops like daincha and sun hemp in the interspaces of beds, along with ginger and harvest the green manure crop during second mulching of ginger beds. Application of dried coconut leaves as mulch in ginger beds after removing the petiole at the time of planting is also recommended. Dried coconut leaves after removing the petiole, split into two at midrib @ 8 kg/3x1 m bed or 5400 kg/ha for effective weed control in ginger. Subsequent application of green leaf at 45 & 90 DAP is not required. This environment friendly technology recorded 87% weed control efficiency, less incidence of soft rot incidence (8.7%) with a B:C ratio 1.97 compared to unweeded control with no mulching.',
        plant_protection='For managing shoot borer following measures are recommended: Spray Beauveria bassiana @ 5 x 10^8 conidia/ml. Spray neem oil (5 ml/l of water) in combination with the cultural control recommended for shoot borer.\n\nFor the management of rhizome rot, bacterial wilt and fusarium yellows of ginger the following practices are recommended:\n• Soil application of Bleaching powder (15g) + lime (250g/3m2) - ATP, 2MAP, 4MAP\n• Soil application of bleaching powder (15 g) + lime (250 g/3 m2 bed) - 2 MAP & 4 MAP\n• Seed treatment/Soil application of Pseudomonas fluorescens (2%) + cowdung supernatant (2%) - ATP, 2 MAP, 4MAP\n• PGPM - Seed treatment/soil application - ATP, 2MAP, 4MAP\n\nAdopt the following to control Rhizome rot and Fusarium yellow:\n• Select sites having proper drainage.\n• Select seed rhizomes from disease free areas.\n• When incidence of rhizome rot is noted in the field, dig out the affected plants and drench the beds with cheshunt compound or 1.0 per cent Bordeaux mixture.\n• Inoculation with native arbuscular mycorrhiza, Trichoderma and Pseudomonas fluorescens at the time of planting is recommended as a biocontrol measure.\n\nIntegrated management of bacterial wilt in ginger:\n• Use healthy seed rhizomes procured from disease-free fields. In endemic areas, avoid fields used for growing solanaceous crops and adopt crop rotation with non-host plants.\n• Select well-drained soils for planting and provide adequate drainage to prevent water logging.\n• Take up planting early (during April in Kerala) and adopt strict phytosanitation like avoiding use of contaminated tools and irrigation water.\n\nTechnology to manage bacterial wilt:\n• Solarize ginger beds using transparent polythene sheets of 100 micron thickness for 45-50 days with an average sunshine of 8 hours per day.\n• Drench the solarized beds with calcium chloride (3%) at the time of planting and repeat the drenching after 30, 45, 60 and 90 days of planting. OR\n• Treat the ginger rhizomes with Bacillus licheniformis (commercial product Bacillich 2%) @ 2 kg/100 litres of water. Drench the soil with the bacterial suspension (1%) (1 kg/100 litres water) at the time of planting and at 30, 45, 60 and 90 days @ 5 litres/3x1 m2 size bed depending on the disease incidence.\n\n6. For controlling the leaf spot disease, 1 per cent Bordeaux mixture may be sprayed.\n7. For control of nematode in endemic area, apply neem cake @ 1.0 t/ha at the time of planting followed by application of neem cake @ 1.0 t/ha at 45 days after planting (DAP).',
        harvesting='For vegetable ginger, the crop can be harvested from sixth month onwards. For dry ginger, harvest the crop between 245-260 days. After harvest, the fibrous roots attached to the rhizomes are trimmed off and soil is removed by washing. Rhizomes are soaked in water overnight and then cleaned. The skin is removed by scrapping with sharp bamboo splits or such other materials. Never use metallic substances since they will discolour the rhizomes. After scrapping, the rhizomes are sun dried for a week with frequent turnings. They are again rubbed well by hand to remove any outer skin.',
        expected_yield='',
        sections=[{'body': 'Ginger (Zingiber officinale)\n\nGinger is a tropical plant adapted for cultivation even in regions of subtropical climate such as the high ranges. It prefers a rich soil with high humus content. Being an exhausting crop, ginger is not cultivated continuously in the same field but shifting cultivation is practised. The crop cannot withstand waterlogging and hence soils with good drainage are preferred for its cultivation. It is shade tolerant/loving crop with shallow roots and therefore suitable for intercropping and as a component in the homesteads where low to medium shade is available.', 'heading': 'Overview'}, {'body': 'Clear the field during February-March and burn the weeds, stubbles, roots etc. in situ. Prepare the land by ploughing or digging. Prepare beds of convenient length (across the slope where the land is undulating), 1 m width, 25 cm height with 40 cm spacing between the beds. Provide drainage channels, one for every 25 beds on flat lands.', 'heading': 'Preparation of land'}, {'body': 'Ginger rhizomes are used for planting. For selection and preservation of seeds, adopt the following methods: Mark healthy and disease free plants in the field when the crop is 6-8 months old and still green. Select best rhizomes free from pest and disease from the marked plants. Handle seed rhizomes carefully to avoid damage to buds. Soak the selected rhizomes for 30 minutes in a solution of mancozeb and malathion to give terminal concentration of 0.3 per cent for the former and 0.1 per cent for the latter. Dry the treated rhizomes in shade by spreading on the floor. Store the treated rhizomes in pits dug under shade, the floor of which is lined with sand or saw dust. It is advisable to spread layers of leaves of Glycosmis pentaphylla (panal). Cover the pits with coconut fronds. Examine the stored rhizomes at monthly intervals and remove the rhizomes that show signs of rotting. This will help to keep the inoculum level low. Provide one or two holes for better aeration. Treat the seed rhizomes similarly before planting also.\n\nSingle bud sprout transplanting: A transplanting technique using single bud sprouts (about 5g) has been standardized to produce good quality planting material with reduced cost. The process include: Selection of healthy rhizomes of high yielding varieties of ginger. Treating the seed rhizome with mancozeb (0.3%) and quinalphos (0.075%) for 30 min before storage. Preparation of single bud sprouts (4-6 g) by cutting ginger rhizomes and Treatment of single bud sprouts with mancozeb 0.3%, for 30 minutes before planting after 30 days. For raising sprouts, 98 well pro-trays and nursery medium of partially decomposed coir pith and vermicompost in the ratio of 3:1 are recommended. The advantages are less planting material requirement, 500-750 kg per ha, 98-100 per cent field establishment, high cost:benefit ratio and suitability for early/delayed planting and high production technology.', 'heading': 'Planting material'}, {'body': 'Remove weeds by hand weeding before each mulching. Repeat weeding according to weed growth during the fifth and sixth month after planting. Earth up the crop during the first mulching and avoid water stagnation.', 'heading': 'After cultivation'}, {'body': 'Ginger oil: Ginger oil is prepared commercially by steam distillation of dried powdered ginger. The yield of oil varies from 1.3 to 3.0 per cent. The major use of ginger oil is as a flavouring agent for beverages, both alcoholic and non-alcoholic.\n\nGinger oleoresin: Oleoresin from ginger is obtained conventionally by extraction of dried powdered ginger with organic solvents like ethyl acetate, ethanol or acetone. Commercial dried ginger yields 3.5-10.0 per cent oleoresin. Ginger oleoresin is a dark brown viscous liquid responsible for the flavour and pungency of the spice.', 'heading': 'Oils and oleoresin'}],
        source_reference='KAU Package of Practices Recommendations: Crops 2024 (16th ed.)',
        source_page_range='152-155',
        is_active=True,
    ),
    dict(
        crop_name='Gladiolus',
        crop_group='Ornamentals',
        season='Best season for planting is September-November.',
        varieties=[{'name': 'Common varieties', 'description': 'Friendship, Spic and Span, Mansoer Red, Dr. Fleming, Peter Pears and White Friendship.'}, {'name': 'Varieties evolved in India', 'description': 'Sapna, Poonam, Nazrana, Apsara, Agnirekha, Mayur, Suchithra, Manmohan, Manohar, Muktha, Archana, Arun and Shobha.'}],
        spacing='The corms are planted at a distance of 30 cm and at 5 cm depth. About one lakh plants can be accommodated in a hectare.',
        manuring_fertilizer='FYM @ 25 t ha-1 is applied and mixed well with the soil. Ridges are made 20 cm apart. Fertilizer application is made @ 50:60:60 kg N:P2O5:K2O per ha. Top dress the crop with 50 kg N, 45 days after planting and earth up.',
        plant_protection='Several species of aphids like green peach aphid, potato aphid and melon aphid damage developing foliage and flowers.\n\nBrown and Fusarium wilts are the major diseases. Spraying the crop with 0.3 per cent copper oxy chloride is recommended when diseases are noticed.',
        harvesting='The plant starts flowering in 2-3 months after planting based on the variety. The entire spike along with two leaves is cut, when the basal flower bud starts opening. Nearly 2 lakh flower spikes can be obtained from one hectare. After harvest of flowers, the plants are left undisturbed in the field. When they start yellowing, the plants are uprooted for harvest of corms and cormels.',
        expected_yield='Nearly 2 lakh flower spikes per hectare.',
        sections=[{'body': 'Gladiolus is grown for its attractive flower spike having florets of huge form, dazzling colour and varying sizes, with long vase life.', 'heading': 'Overview'}, {'body': 'It can be grown in a wide range of soils, light sandy to clay loam. Deep well-drained acidic soils with a pH of 5.5-6.5 are the best for cultivation.', 'heading': 'Soil'}, {'body': 'Gladiolus is propagated by corms and cormels. Size of the corm markedly influences the growth and flowering of gladiolus. Medium and large sized corms are preferred for planting, as small corms produce only small flower spikes.', 'heading': 'Propagation'}, {'body': 'The land is ploughed two or three times and FYM @ 25 t ha-1 is applied and mixed well with the soil.', 'heading': 'Planting'}, {'body': 'The crop has to be irrigated once in two or three days depending upon soil and weather conditions.', 'heading': 'Irrigation'}],
        source_reference='KAU Package of Practices Recommendations: Crops 2024 (16th ed.)',
        source_page_range='266-267 (book pp 254-255)',
        is_active=True,
    ),
