    dict(
        crop_name='Irul',
        crop_group='Agroforestry',
        season='',
        varieties=[],
        spacing='Planting in the field is done at a spacing of 4 m x 4 m. Standard pits are filled with rich top soil and FYM 5 kg.',
        manuring_fertilizer='From second or third year onwards, 50-70 g N, 50-60 g P2O5 and 50-70 g K2O is also added to each plant depending on size.',
        plant_protection='In fire damaged areas, xylia trees are found to be infected by fungi like Fomes, Polystictus etc. Stem borers and defoliators also cause severe damage. Growing of trees in healthy environmental conditions, preventing fire, grazing etc. is found to be more effective than using pesticides, fungicides etc.',
        harvesting='Growth is fast during favourable season, attaining a diameter increment of about 6 cm/year. Felling can be done after a period of 30-50 years growth.',
        expected_yield='',
        sections=[{'body': 'Irul (Xylia xylocarpa) is locally also known as Irumullu, Kadamaram, Iruppool, Panka, Pankali etc. These large sized trees are commonly seen in the deciduous forests, mostly found in places located about 600 m above sea level. It grows well in places where annual rainfall is above 1000 mm and atmosphere is hot as well as humid. River banks and places with red soil are best suited for its growth.', 'heading': 'Overview'}, {'body': 'This is effected by direct sowing or by planting stumps and seedlings from nursery or seedlings from forest. Sun dried seeds are used for sowing in the field, found to be the most efficient method. Soaking the seeds in cold water overnight enhances germination. Soaking the seeds in growth hormones like IAA 250 ppm or GA 50 ppm for 30 minutes will enhance germination to 70 per cent.', 'heading': 'Propagation'}, {'body': 'Reddish brown heartwood possesses white lines. One cubic meter timber weighs about 832-944 kg. Wood is termite resistant. Due to the presence of resin in the wood, it can resist attack of fungi and termites. Hopea timber was used in the manufacture of railway sleepers, pillars of huge buildings, bridges etc. Bark is having medicinal value: powdered bark mixed with honey is a good medicine for diarrhoea, vomiting, gonorrhoea etc.', 'heading': 'Timber and uses'}, {'body': 'These large sized trees are commonly seen in the deciduous forests, mostly found in places located about 600 m above sea level; a large number of trees having 30 m height and 2.5 m diameter can be seen in the forest areas of Malabar. Profusely branched stem is its characteristic feature; reddish ash bark is very rough and cracky. In dry areas stunted growth is reported; grows well in places where annual rainfall is above 1000 mm and atmosphere is hot as well as humid. River banks and places with red soil are best suited; lateritic soil is also found to be good, but poor growth occurs in clay soil.', 'heading': 'Description and distribution'}, {'body': 'A large number of seedlings are naturally found growing in forests; they are fire and drought resistant. At the end of the winter season, leaf fall starts; soon after, new sprouts and flowers are produced. Light yellow flowers possess a good smell. Pods are 10-15 cm long and 4-5 cm diameter, brownish red and shoe-shaped, with 8-10 seeds present in one pod (1 kg pod contains more than 3000 seeds). During early growth, shady regions are preferred; later they become light demanding. Root suckers are abundantly produced in certain regions; it is also a very good coppicer.', 'heading': 'Natural regeneration and growth habit'}],
        source_reference='KAU Package of Practices Recommendations: Crops 2024 (16th ed.)',
        source_page_range='35-36 of Agroforestry chapter (book pp 310-312)',
        is_active=True,
    ),
    dict(
        crop_name='Jack',
        crop_group='Fruits',
        season='Plant one year old grafts at the onset of monsoon showers.',
        varieties=[{'name': 'Muttom varikka', 'description': 'A firm fleshed, sweet scented variety.'}, {'name': 'Singapore or Srilanka jack', 'description': 'An introduced variety from Srilanka. It bears fruits in 3 years after planting and is extremely precocious in habit. The fruits are more or less the same size as the common jack fruit. A tree may yield as many as 250 fruits.'}, {'name': 'Sindhoor', 'description': 'Highly sweet variety with attractive sunset orange coloured flakes. Bear medium sized fruits (11-12 kg) twice a year.'}, {'name': 'Pechiparai 1', 'description': 'A firm fleshed variety suitable for table purpose, cooking and also for chips. Flakes are creamish white with an average fruit weight of 9-10 Kg.'}],
        spacing='Prepare pits of size 60 cm x 60 cm x 60 cm at a spacing of 12-15 m.',
        manuring_fertilizer='Fill pits with mixture of topsoil and 10 kg compost or FYM per pit to a level higher than the adjoining ground. Jack is rarely manured. Even without fertilizer application, the jack trees come up well under Kerala conditions.',
        plant_protection='The important pests of jack are shoot borer caterpillar, mealy bug and jack scale.\n\nThe common diseases that attack the tree are the pink disease, stem rot and fruit rot. Pruning of affected plants and protecting the cut ends with Bordeaux paste are recommended against these diseases.',
        harvesting='The seedlings generally bear after eight years and the grafted plants after three years of planting. The fruiting season lasts about four months from January-February to May-June. The average yield from one tree is about 50-100 fruits per year.',
        expected_yield='50-100 fruits per tree per year.',
        sections=[{'body': 'Jack comes up well in humid regions up to an elevation of 1000 m. Soil should be deep and well drained. Any rise in water table or poor aeration of the soil is detrimental to the crop.', 'heading': 'Overview'}, {'body': 'Jackfruit differs in size, shape and quality. The jackfruit may be classified into two groups: (i) soft fleshed (koozha) and (ii) firm fleshed (varikka). The firm fleshed type is highly tasty, sweet and crisp. The two groups are further classified depending on the taste, size of fruit, odour of flesh and also the nature, shape and diversity of prickles on the rind.', 'heading': 'Varieties/Types'}, {'body': 'Use grafts for planting. For grafting, raise seedlings in polythene bags and when they are 9-12 months old, do inarching. One month after grafting, behead the rootstock above the graft joint.\n\nEpicotyl grafting can be undertaken successfully in jack. Three to four month old, 10 cm long scions are grafted on five days old rootstocks in polythene bags by the cleft method during the month of June and keep under moist conditions. The scions should be pre-cured 10 days before grafting by clipping the leaf blades and keeping the petioles intact on the twig. The graft union establishes by 80 days after grafting operations.\n\nA new method of approach grafting utilizing sprouted jack seedlings (maximum 20 days old) as root stock and tender shoot tips of 2 to 3 weeks old with one or two tender leaves as scion is successful, fast and cost affective.', 'heading': 'Planting materials'}, {'body': 'Plant the grafts in the same depth as they were in the containers, preferably in the late evening. Deep planting results in poor growth of the graft. Ensure that the graft joint is above the soil level. Stake the plants to prevent snapping at the graft joints. Excellent drainage and adequate watering result in better performance. At no stage it should be exposed to drought or frost. It is useful to provide some protection, especially to young trees.', 'heading': 'Planting'}, {'body': 'A process protocol developed for canning of tender jack fruit involves treatment combinations like blanching process, thermal processing parameters viz; time, temperature and preservatives were optimized based on the quality analysis and shelf life studies. The study concluded that the samples which were blanched for one minute at 100oC and pasteurized at 90oC for F value 10 were found to be superior. A shelf life of 2 years could be achieved for pasteurised and sterilised tender jack fruit at refrigerated storage and ambient condition respectively, using this new technology. The product resembled the fresh sample, available to the consumers in a ready to cook form throughout the year.', 'heading': 'Ready to cook tender jack fruit'}],
        source_reference='KAU Package of Practices Recommendations: Crops 2024 (16th ed.)',
        source_page_range='249-250 (book pp 235-236)',
        is_active=True,
    ),
    dict(
        crop_name='Jasmine',
        crop_group='Ornamentals',
        season='Planting is usually done during June-August.',
        varieties=[{'name': 'Jasminum sambac', 'description': 'Gundumalli, Motia, Virupakshi, Sujimalli, Madanabanam, Ramabanam.'}, {'name': 'Jasminum grandiflorum', 'description': 'Co-1 Pitchi, Co-2 Pitchi, Thimmapuram, Lucknow.'}, {'name': 'Jasminum auriculatum', 'description': 'Co-1 Mulla, Co-2 Mulla, Long Point, Long Round, Short Point, Short Round.'}],
        spacing='Planting distance depends on the species: J. sambac 1.2 m x 1.2 m; J. auriculatum 1.8 m x 1.8 m; J. grandiflorum 2.0 m x 1.5 m.',
        manuring_fertilizer='Each plant requires a fertilizer dose of 120 g N, 240 g P2O5 and 240 g K2O. The fertilizers are mixed together and applied in two split doses during January and July. This has to be supplemented with organic manures like neem cake, groundnut oil cake etc. @ 100 g per plant per month.',
        plant_protection='Diseases: Fusarium wilt - Prevented by drenching the soil with 1 per cent Bordeaux mixture.',
        harvesting='',
        expected_yield='',
        sections=[{'body': 'Jasmine is an important flower crop that could be grown on a commercial scale in Kerala. Jasminum sambac is the most ideal species for cultivation in Kerala. The flowers are used for preparing garlands. The jasmine oil has great export potential in addition to its use for medicinal purpose. There are trailing, climbing and erect growing species and cultivars.', 'heading': 'Overview'}, {'body': 'Yield of flowers and jasmine oil vary according to the species and management practices: J. sambac - 5 t ha-1 flower yield, 15.44 kg ha-1 oil yield. J. auriculatum - 5 t ha-1 flower yield, 28.00 kg ha-1 oil yield. J. grandiflorum - 6 t ha-1 flower yield, 29.00 kg ha-1 oil yield.', 'heading': 'Yield'}, {'body': 'Jasmine can be planted on a wide range of soils. Well-drained sandy loams and red loams are ideal for its cultivation. In clayey soils, there is increased vegetative growth and reduced flowering. They give good yield in low rainfall conditions.', 'heading': 'Soil and climate'}, {'body': 'Layering and cutting are the main propagation methods. Better rooting of cuttings can be obtained by planting in coarse sand and also by using any of the rooting hormones like IBA (5000 ppm), IAA (1000 ppm) and NAA (5000 ppm). Simple and compound layering methods are followed during June-July to October-November. Layers will be ready for planting within 90-120 days.', 'heading': 'Propagation'}, {'body': 'After ploughing the land, pits of about 40 cm x 40 cm x 40 cm size are taken and filled with topsoil and 15 kg well rotten FYM. Planting distance depends on the species and also on soil and environmental conditions.', 'heading': 'Planting'}, {'body': 'Pruning is essential and is done at a height of 45 cm from the ground level during mid December-January.', 'heading': 'Pruning'}, {'body': 'Manual weeding is usually done which is effective but expensive. Mulching also reduces weed population.', 'heading': 'Weed control'}, {'body': 'Constant and adequate water supply during peak flowering season (March-October) is essential for high yield of flowers. After flowering is over, water supply can be cut off. During summer, irrigate twice a week.', 'heading': 'Irrigation'}],
        source_reference='KAU Package of Practices Recommendations: Crops 2024 (16th ed.)',
        source_page_range='265-266 (book pp 252-254)',
        is_active=True,
    ),
    dict(
        crop_name='Jeevakom',
        crop_group='Medicinal and Aromatic Plants',
        season='Crop is raised as rainfed. Best time for planting is after the receipt of summer showers (May-June).',
        varieties=[{'name': 'Local cultivars', 'description': 'Only local cultivars (ecotypes) are available for cultivation.'}],
        spacing='Bulbils are planted at a spacing of 10x10 cm.',
        manuring_fertilizer='Crop responds well to organic manures. Powdered FYM/leaf compost/vermicompost is applied @ 100 g/m2, 60 days and 90 days after planting.',
        plant_protection='',
        harvesting='After six months of planting, aerial parts start yellowing. Leaves turn yellow and ultimately dry up. Bulbils are gathered from the beds manually. Bulbils covered with the leaf sheaths are separated, roots removed. Mother and daughter bulbils are separated. Since the part used as drug and the propagule are one and the same, the mother bulbils are used as drug and the side bulbils can be used as planting material for next season. Total yield from a bed is 6.0 kg/3m2. The bulbils which are succulent in mature can be stored in earthern containers in a cool room for a period of 4-5 months without loosing viability.',
        expected_yield='6.0 kg per 3 m2 bed.',
        sections=[{'body': "Jeevakom is a medicinal orchid widely used in Ayurveda. It is available in the market in two forms, 'Jeevakom' and 'Edavakom'. Jeevakom belongs to the 'astavarga' group of drugs mentioned in Ayurveda. It is a short-stemmed fibrous rooted lithophytic herb with 5-7 leaves. Stem is swollen at the base forming a conical pseudobalbil which is the part used in medicine. Jeevakom is a rejuvenating drug and an ingredient of many Ayurvedic formulations viz. Chavanaprash, Dhanwantharam kashayam, Dhanwantharam kuzhambu and Ashtavargam kashayam.", 'heading': 'Overview'}, {'body': 'The plant is present in select pockets of India, mainly in the forests. In Kerala it occurs at altitudes ranging from 650 to 1000 m above MSL. In the forests jeevakom colonizes on decomposed organic matter on wet rocks amidst moss and grass. It is a shade loving plant and prefers 50%-75% shade for better growth, yield and quality. The crop can be grown under natural as well as artificial shade provided by shade net.', 'heading': 'Climate and soil'}, {'body': 'The plant is propagated through pseudo bulbils. Mother bulbils cut into single node bits can also be used.', 'heading': 'Propagation'}, {'body': 'Select an area receiving 50-75 percent shade (natural or artificial shade provided by shade net). Plough the field to good tilth. Incorporate dried FYM at the rate of 20 t/ha-1. Prepare raised beds of convenient length and breadth to facilitate drainage.', 'heading': 'Land preparation'}, {'body': 'Stored pseudobulbils of previous year are used as planting material. Small side bulbils weighing 1 g or mother bulbils cut into 2 g bits having single node can be used. Seed rate is 250 kg/ha-1.', 'heading': 'Seed rate'}, {'body': 'Bulbils are placed horizontally over the beds, they are just pressed gently into the soil. Beds are mulched heavily after planting with green or partially decomposed dry leaves.', 'heading': 'Planting'}, {'body': 'Carry out gap filling if any within 30 days. Remove the weeds as and when they come up. The crop performs well under moist condition. Cover the soil with mulch for the first 4-5 months, keep the soil moist always. Crop flowers 1-2 months after planting. Side bulbils start emerging from 4th month onwards.', 'heading': 'After cultivation'}],
        source_reference='KAU Package of Practices Recommendations: Crops 2024 (16th ed.)',
        source_page_range='284-285 (book pp 270-272)',
        is_active=True,
    ),
    dict(
        crop_name='Kacholam',
        crop_group='Medicinal and Aromatic Plants',
        season='Planting is done during the month of May with the receipt of pre-monsoon showers.',
        varieties=[{'name': 'Rajani and Kasthuri', 'description': 'High yielding varieties with a yield potential of more than 2 tonnes dry rhizomes per ha and have good aroma and flavour. Local types are also under cultivation.'}],
        spacing='Take small pits in the beds in rows with a spacing of 20 cm x 15 cm and at a depth of 4-5 cm and plant rhizomes with at least one viable healthy bud facing upwards. Adopt seed rate of 700-800 kg ha-1.',
        manuring_fertilizer='Apply FYM or compost as basal dose @ 20 t ha-1, either by broadcasting and ploughing or by covering the rhizome in pits after planting. Apply N, P2O5 and K2O @ 50, 50 and 50 kg ha-1 at the time of the first and second weeding.',
        plant_protection='During heavy rains, leaf rot disease occurs in certain localities. For controlling this disease, drench the beds with 1 per cent Bordeaux mixture.\n\nFor controlling nematodes (Meloidogyne incognita and Radopholus similis) associated with Kacholam, rhizome treatment with Pseudomonas fluorescense @ 3 per cent weight by weight of seed material or by green leaf mulching with neem and glyricidia @ 5 kg/m2 at 30 DAP can be recommended.',
        harvesting='The crop can be harvested seven months after planting. Drying of the leaves is the indication of crop maturity for harvest. Harvest the crop carefully without damaging the rhizomes, remove dried leaves and roots, wash the rhizome in water and dry. With sharp knife, chop the rhizomes into circular pieces of uniform size except the end portion, which has to be cut separately. Spread the cut rhizomes uniformly on clean floor and allow drying for four days. On fourth day, heap the rhizomes and keep it overnight. On the next day it is again spread and dried. Clean the dried produce, bag and store in cool dry place or market it. Prolonged storage can cause insect and fungus attack.',
        expected_yield='',
        sections=[{'body': 'An attractive medicinal plant used in various medicines. The aromatic essential oil of the rhizome is widely used in perfumery, as a condiment and as a folk medicine. Asians employ the rhizomes and leaves as a perfume in cosmetics, hair washes and powders. Rhizomes are used to protect the clothing against insects and are chewed with betel nut. Kacholam is a plant adapted for tropical climate. Fertile loamy soil having good drainage is ideal for the crop. Laterite soil with heavy organic manure application is also well suited.', 'heading': 'Overview'}, {'body': 'Prepare the land to a good tilth during March by ploughing or digging. On receipt of pre-monsoon showers in April, prepare beds of 1 m width 25 cm height and of convenient length with spacing of 40 cm between beds.', 'heading': 'Preparation of land'}, {'body': 'Whole or split rhizome with at least one healthy sprout is the planting material in kacholam. Select well developed healthy and disease free rhizomes. Rhizomes can be stored in cool dry place or pits dug under shade, plastered with mud or cowdung. Two weeks before planting of the new crop, smoking the rhizomes by spreading it on Glycosmis pentaphylla (panal) leaves is practised in certain localities.', 'heading': 'Seed materials'}, {'body': 'After planting, mulch the beds with dry or green leaves @ 15 t ha-1.', 'heading': 'Mulching'}, {'body': 'Remove weeds as and when necessary. Apply fertilizers and earth up the crop during the first and second weeding (45 and 90 days after planting). Avoid water stagnation in the beds. Further weeding is not necessary as the spreading leaves smother the soil surface.', 'heading': 'After cultivation'}],
        source_reference='KAU Package of Practices Recommendations: Crops 2024 (16th ed.)',
        source_page_range='274-275 (book pp 260-261)',
        is_active=True,
    ),
    dict(
        crop_name='Kampakam',
        crop_group='Agroforestry',
        season='',
        varieties=[],
        spacing='Planting is done in pits of 45 cm cube with the onset of monsoon at a spacing of 4 m x 4 m filled with 5 kg FYM.',
        manuring_fertilizer='From second year onwards depending on size of plant, 50-70 g N, 40-50 g P2O5, 40-60 g K2O along with 30 kg FYM are applied to each plant.',
        plant_protection='Usually the tree is affected by fungi like Fomes lamaocnis, Tramelies spongi pellis etc. which will cause rotting. Leaf spot by Botryodiplodia theobrommae is serious in some parts. Plants are also affected by stem borers like Xyleborus, Mussicus etc.',
        harvesting='If the area is fertile, within 40 years, timber of above 20 m height and 150 cm diameter will be obtained.',
        expected_yield='',
        sections=[{'body': 'In Kerala, Kampakam (Hopea parviflora) is also known as Thampakam, Irippu, Irumpakam etc. This round headed huge tree is found in the wet evergreen forests of Western Ghats. Full grown tree usually attains 30-37 m height and 4-4.5 m breadth at the end of 40 years. Heavy rainfall and properly drained and aerated soil are necessary for its growth.', 'heading': 'Overview'}, {'body': 'Artificial propagation is by seeds. Seeds can be stored in sacks up to 20 days; 1 kg contains about 2500 seeds. Both direct sowing or transferring nursery raised seedlings to fields are practiced. It is good to sow seeds soon after collection as they are recalcitrant.', 'heading': 'Propagation'}, {'body': 'Timber has more strength, weight and hardness than teak. One cubic meter timber weighs about 945 kg. As bark contains tannin, it is used in the tanning industry. Termite and pest infections are rare, so it is used in manufacture of furniture, railway sleepers, buildings etc.', 'heading': 'Timber and uses'}, {'body': 'This round headed huge tree is found in the wet evergreen forests of Western Ghats, mostly distributed in areas located between sea level and 1100 m above it. Seen abundantly along river banks and hill slopes with fertile soil.', 'heading': 'Description'}, {'body': 'Artificial propagation is by seeds, which can be stored in sacks up to 20 days (1 kg contains about 2500 seeds). It is good to sow seeds soon after collection as they are recalcitrant; in direct sowing, shady and weed free localities are preferable, and shade trees should be removed after 4-5 years of growth. After sowing, soil covering of thickness not more than 4 mm is given; germination takes place within 2-3 weeks. Natural regeneration is seen in forests by germination of wind dispersed seeds during rainy season; only in sunny regions do seedlings grow well. Dense vegetation will destroy its seedlings; it is sensitive to fire and frost.', 'heading': 'Propagation and natural regeneration'}, {'body': 'Even though Kampakam is an evergreen tree, it will drop mature leaves during December and April. Pale yellowish flowers are produced during January to February. Seeds attain maturity during May-June. They can overcome drought as they are deep rooted and prefer wet areas. Although shade tolerant during early stages, it later becomes a light demander.', 'heading': 'Growth habit'}],
        source_reference='KAU Package of Practices Recommendations: Crops 2024 (16th ed.)',
        source_page_range='33-34 of Agroforestry chapter (book pp 309-310)',
        is_active=True,
    ),
    dict(
        crop_name='Kanjiram',
        crop_group='Medicinal and Aromatic Plants',
        season='Seedlings can be planted in main field with the onset of South-West monsoon in May-June.',
        varieties=[],
        spacing='The saplings are later transplanted to the main field on to pits of about 1 m x 1 m taken at a spacing of 6 m x 6 m, filled with top soil and organic manure.',
        manuring_fertilizer='FYM at 2 kg per tree is to be applied during early stages and the dose is gradually increased to 20 kg from 5th year onwards.',
        plant_protection='No serious pests or diseases are generally noted in this crop.',
        harvesting='Flowering is during March-April and fruiting during May-December. Fruits take about 8-9 months to mature. Mature pods are collected and seeds are extracted, washed, dried and stored for later use.',
        expected_yield='50-75 kg dry seed per tree per year.',
        sections=[{'body': 'Strychnos or kanjiram is a large deciduous tree with a fairly straight and cylindrical bole having dark grey or yellowish grey bark and minute tubercles (Strychnos nux-vomica). Strychnos is highly toxic to man and animals producing stiffness of muscles and convulsions, ultimately leading to death. In small doses it can serve as efficacious cure for paralysis and other nervous disorders. Root and root bark is used in fever and dysentery.', 'heading': 'Overview'}, {'body': 'The plant is distributed throughout India in deciduous forests up to 1200 m. It prefers tropical and subtropical climate. It is grown in different soil types such as laterite, sandy and alluvial.', 'heading': 'Climate and soil'}, {'body': 'It is propagated through seeds. Germination can be substantially increased by treating the seeds with hot water (50oC) for a period of six to twelve hours prior to sowing.', 'heading': 'Propagation'}, {'body': 'Seeds are sown in poly bags.', 'heading': 'Planting'}, {'body': 'The basins of the trees are cleared of weeds and after application of manures and fertilizers covered with soil.', 'heading': 'After cultivation'}],
        source_reference='KAU Package of Practices Recommendations: Crops 2024 (16th ed.)',
        source_page_range='287 (book pp 274)',
        is_active=True,
    ),
