        sections=[{'body': 'Venga (Pterocarpus marsupium) is one of the largest trees of deciduous forests. Highly branched (spreading) crown is its characteristic feature. Reddish gum is seen flowing through the timber. It attains a height of about 30 m and diameter of about 2.5 m. Grown in steep hill slopes and plain areas; in Kerala, found in regions located about 1070 m above sea level. Regions having rainfall 75-200 cm are best suited. This light dependent tree becomes frost sensitive during the later stages of growth.', 'heading': 'Overview'}, {'body': 'Artificial propagation is by seeds, collected from full grown trees and soaked in cold water for about 2-3 days before sowing. Germination starts within 10-15 days. Seedlings of 10 cm height are transplanted in polythene bags filled with potting mixture and kept under partial shade in nursery. One kg of seed lot contains about 1000 seeds.', 'heading': 'Propagation'}, {'body': 'Sapwood is pale white; yellowish brown heartwood possesses dark lines. 1 m3 timber weighs about 800 kg. This tree is best suited for agroforestry and social forestry, used as a shade tree in the tea and coffee plantations of South India. Gum-keno obtained from Pterocarpus is used for dyeing, printing, tanning etc. Leaves are used as cattle feed and green manure. Timber is used for the manufacture of posts, buildings, beams, furniture, boat, agricultural instruments etc.', 'heading': 'Timber and uses'}, {'body': 'Venga is one of the largest trees of deciduous forests. Highly branched (spreading) crown is its characteristic feature. Reddish gum is seen flowing through the timber.', 'heading': 'Description'}],
